s by  like Chavez in Venezuela Gloria and  Ecuador Morales and Bolivia and the like  is it a lot of the time business  people's approach to understanding these  expropriations is to look at them and  say you know what's going on is we got a  government that's ideological they're  just completely oblivious to have basic  economics and they're essentially crazy  like a lot of business people talk about  populist politicians in these ways I  think it is true that the politicians  often are quite ideological but I think  it's also a serious strategic mistake to  think of them as being crazy and when I  talk to executives who've been involved  on the losing end in various  expropriations they often recognize  after the fact that the politicians were  following a logic in what they were  doing and it's a logic that is different  from the usual logics of business you  know politicians need to think about who  is my core support base what is going to  keep me in power and what are my  possibilities within existing and  potential  future political institutions and if we  think about politicians systematically  in those ways we often see that there  seemingly bits crazy behavior actually  makes more sense than it does if we just  you know take it from the perspective of  oh they're doing something that I don't  like and that I think is crazy and when  we do this we see that it's actually  possible for companies to formulate  strategies to deal with the threat of  expropriation instead of proactively  mitigate those risks not eliminate them  entirely but to state take steps that  reduce the sort of problems that they  might encounter so the third example I'm  gonna give I'm gonna come back to the  United States and this is the example of  health care reform in the United States  this obviously has been the topic of a  lot of turbulence in a high political  drama you know first in 2009 and 2010  we'll Obamacare chaos will it not pass  then more recently in 2017 and 2018  will i